@page "/about-us"
<PageTitle>Understand The Singularity Workshop</PageTitle>

<div class="workshop-understand">
    <header class="understand-hero">
        <div class="understand-kicker">THE SINGULARITY WORKSHOP // UNDERSTAND</div>
        <h1>We are not building one giant application.</h1>
        <p class="understand-lede">
            We are building a set of small, composable pieces that can be assembled into
            different experiences, run in different hosts, and explained in terms humans can actually follow.
        </p>
        <div class="understand-actions">
            <a class="understand-action primary" href="create">SEE HOW IT COMPOSES</a>
            <a class="understand-action" href="explore">ENTER AN EXPERIENCE</a>
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="understand-section">
        <div class="section-kicker">01 // THE PROBLEM</div>
        <h2>Complex software becomes a monolith surprisingly easily.</h2>
        <p>
            A feature starts inside an application because that is the fastest place to prove it.
            Then another feature needs it. Then another host needs it. Eventually the application
            knows about the data, the renderer knows about the behavior, the UI knows about the
            storage, and changing one thing means touching everything.
        </p>
        <p class="accent-copy">
            The Workshop is an attempt to stop that collapse.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="understand-section">
        <div class="section-kicker">02 // THE ARCHITECTURE</div>
        <h2>Separate the jobs. Compose the result.</h2>

        <div class="architecture-stack">
            <article class="architecture-card core">
                <span class="architecture-index">A</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>FSM_API</strong>
                    <p>State and behavior. Small, deterministic building blocks for lifecycle and execution.</p>
                </div>
                <a href="https://github.com/TrentBest/FSM_API" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SOURCE ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="architecture-card compose">
                <span class="architecture-index">B</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>FSM_COS</strong>
                    <p>Composition. A manifest asks for capabilities; independent MicroBundles are loaded, arbitrated, and sealed into a runtime assembly.</p>
                </div>
                <a href="https://github.com/TrentBest/TheSingularityWorkshop.FSM_COS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SOURCE ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="architecture-card bundle">
                <span class="architecture-index">C</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>MICROBUNDLES</strong>
                    <p>Capabilities. A bundle can describe behavior or semantic effects without becoming a WebPage, WPF, or Unity application.</p>
                </div>
                <a href="https://github.com/TrentBest/TheSingularityWorkshop.MicroBundleDomain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOMAIN ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="architecture-card data">
                <span class="architecture-index">D</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>REPOSITORIES + WAREHOUSE</strong>
                    <p>Artifacts and persistent reality are separate concerns: repositories deliver bundles; SingularityWarehouse carries identity, mappings, provenance, telemetry, and domain data.</p>
                </div>
                <a href="https://github.com/TrentBest/SingularityWarehouse" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WAREHOUSE ↗</a>
            </article>

            <article class="architecture-card semantic">
                <span class="architecture-index">E</span>
                <div>
                    <strong>PROTOCOL + GRAMMAR</strong>
                    <p>Human-readable meaning can cross a deterministic boundary into compact, machine-addressable identity and structure.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="architecture-links">
                    <a href="https://github.com/TrentBest/TheSingularityWorkshop.ProtocolAi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PROTOCOL ↗</a>
                    <a href="https://github.com/TrentBest/TheSingularityWorkshop.GrammarAi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GRAMMAR ↗</a>
                </div>
            </article>
        </div>

        <div class="assembly-line" aria-label="Workshop composition flow">
            <span>MANIFEST</span><b>→</b><span>CAPABILITIES</span><b>→</b><span>ARBITRATION</span><b>→</b><span>RUNTIME</span><b>→</b><span>EXPERIENCE</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="understand-section impact-section">
        <div class="section-kicker">03 // WHY IT MATTERS</div>
        <h2>What this changes for you</h2>

        <div class="impact-grid">
            <article>
                <span class="impact-number">01</span>
                <h3>Developers</h3>
                <p>Use the small package you actually need instead of adopting an entire application framework.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span class="impact-number">02</span>
                <h3>Teams</h3>
                <p>Separate domain work from presentation work. A capability can be developed once and manifested in more than one host.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span class="impact-number">03</span>
                <h3>AI systems</h3>
                <p>Give probabilistic intelligence a deterministic vocabulary to operate against instead of asking it to invent executable syntax.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span class="impact-number">04</span>
                <h3>Data owners</h3>
                <p>Bring existing data into the ecosystem through adapters and repositories without turning the data source into the composition engine.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span class="impact-number">05</span>
                <h3>Product builders</h3>
                <p>Compose an Experience from capabilities instead of rebuilding the same application architecture for every new product.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <span class="impact-number">06</span>
                <h3>Users</h3>
                <p>Get software that can be assembled around the experience you actually need, rather than navigating a monolith built around somebody else's assumptions.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="understand-section">
        <div class="section-kicker">04 // THE WEBPAGE YOU ARE IN</div>
        <h2>The browser is a manifestation, not the architecture.</h2>
        <p>
            This WebPage is the public laboratory door. It is where the Workshop can demonstrate
            living interfaces, spatial experiences, composition, education, rendering research,
            and other experiments while the underlying packages remain independently useful.
        </p>

        <div class="manifestation-row">
            <div><strong>WEBPAGE</strong><span>browser / Blazor</span></div>
            <div><strong>ANYAPP</strong><span>desktop / local runtime</span></div>
            <div><strong>MYVR</strong><span>future persistent manifestation</span></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="understand-section examples-section">
        <div class="section-kicker">05 // PROOF, NOT PROMISES</div>
        <h2>The site should let you see the machinery.</h2>
        <div class="example-grid">
            <a href="explore"><strong>EXPLORE</strong><span>Walk through a living Workshop environment and inspect what the system can become.</span></a>
            <a href="create"><strong>CREATE</strong><span>See the composition vocabulary: MicroBundles, manifests, arbitration, state, and runtime.</span></a>
            <a href="education"><strong>EDUCATION</strong><span>See the same substrate applied to a shared lesson with multiple participants.</span></a>
            <a href="madmen"><strong>MADMEN</strong><span>See a deliberately different domain exercise using the same Workshop style of separation.</span></a>
        </div>
        <p class="quiet-note">
            Inventor and Revit are deliberately not part of this public opening story right now.
            They remain useful examples of how external data can eventually enter the ecosystem.
        </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="understand-footer">
        <strong>THE WORKSHOP IS THE SYSTEM OF PIECES.</strong>
        <span>THE EXPERIENCE IS WHAT WE COMPOSE FROM THEM.</span>
    </footer>
</div>

